From swilcox at capu.net  Mon Jan  1 08:19:05 2001
From: swilcox at capu.net (Steve Wilcox)
Date: Mon, 01 Jan 2001 03:19:05 -0500
Subject: DLL Linking Problems
Message-ID: <004301c073cb$81dbe0c0$5a8d3240@wilcoxalhrgmve>

Dear S-Colleagues:

I am having trouble linking C code into S-Plus 4.0 for Windows.  I am using
the GNU GCC compiler and am able to produce a DLL, but attempting to load
the DLL in S-Plus does not succeed.  I would very much appreciate a pointer
or two from those who are familiar with the procedure.  Here are the
details:

Working with MINGW32 gcc version 2.95.2 release 19991024 (version using
MSCVRT) from Mumit Khan's website, I am able to create a DLL for the
'convolve' example in Venables & Ripley's "S Programming" book using the
recommended procedure:

gcc -O2 -c convolve.c
dllwrap -o convolve.dll --export-all-symbols convolve.o

These successfully run in the DOS window, but when I attempt to dynamically
load the DLL in S-Plus 4.0 Release 3 running under Windows 2000 (build 2195,
Service Pack 1, RC 1.1), I get a negative return code:

> dll.load('C:\mingw32\gcc-2.95.2\bin',call='cdecl',symbols='convolve')
[1] -32

Another approach also results in the same outcome.  In the CYGWIN-1.1.7-1
bash shell and using gcc-2.95.2-6, it seems necessary to add
the -DBUILDING_DLL=1 option in addition to the recommended -mno-cygwin as
follows:

wilcox steven p@WILCOX-ALHRGMVE ~/c$ gcc -c -mno-cygwin -DBUILDING_DLL=1 -O2
convolve.c
wilcox steven p@WILCOX-ALHRGMVE ~/c$ dllwrap -o
convolve.dll --export-all-symbols convolve.o

The attempt to load the DLL also results in a negative return code:
> dll.load('C:\cygwin\home\wilcox steven
p\c\convolve.dll',call='cdecl',symbols='convolve')
[1] -32

Using a different set of options provided by a makefile called
"Makefile.nocygwin" under CYGWIN, I am able to get a DLL to work with MS
Excel 97, so the problem seems to be on the S-Plus side.  Is there a way to
make dll.load work with the GNU compiler and windows 2000?

With best regards,

   Steven Wilcox, Ph.D.
   swilcox at capu.net

