From JJanis at med.wayne.edu  Tue Jan  2 22:29:08 2001
From: JJanis at med.wayne.edu (Janisse, James)
Date: Tue, 02 Jan 2001 17:29:08 -0500
Subject: What software is faster than S-Plus?
Message-ID: <1D593E45800CD311936B00902740C68C75C55C@medadm-nt.med.wayne.edu>

I am testing a new method for setting acceptable levels of human exposure to
a chemical substance.  This new method relies on a smoothing function to
estimate the dose-response curve.  I am using S-Plus 2000 and Windows 98.
The smooth is fit using the gam function and the acceptable level of
exposure is the predictor score that corresponds to a certain change in
outcome.  Based on my communication with S-PLUS technical support the only
way to solve for a predictor score given an outcome score, when a gam is
used with a smoothing term, is to use one of the minimization functions
(i.e. ms, nls).  This works really well when I only need to solve this once.
It takes about 2 seconds using a 600 Mhz Machine with 128 Meg of RAM.  

The problem is that I want to run a Monte Carlo analysis.  For each cell in
the study I will be running 2000 samples.  For each of those samples I will
be running an additional 100 samples.  Thus there are 200000 samples for
each cell.  The computer time spent doing this will be over 100 hours for
each cell in the study.  Given the number of cells that we are looking at
this would take a few years of straight computer time.

Are there any mathematical software programs available that could do this
type of a task much quicker?  I don't know much about using a C routine to
do this type of task.  Is this a possible avenue?  Does anyone know anything
about other statistical packages like Mathcad or Matlad?

Any assistance would be greatly appreciated.  I don't want to grow old
waiting for my results.

James 

------------------------------------------------------------------------
James J. Janisse
Center for Healthcare Effectiveness Research
Department of Psychology
Wayne State University, Detroit MI USA
------------------------------------------------------------------------

From fharrell at virginia.edu  Tue Jan  2 22:36:33 2001
From: fharrell at virginia.edu (Frank E Harrell Jr)
Date: Tue, 02 Jan 2001 17:36:33 -0500
Subject: S-Plus Report with Postscript Graphics -> PDF using LaTeX
Message-ID: <3A5257F1.F06D074A@virginia.edu>

After grading several final projects from students in my
Statistical Computing and Graphics course and seeing how poorly
Microsoft Word sizes and renders windows metafile graphics, I'm
going to take the plunge next semester and require students to
use LaTeX to produce their reports.  I've always hesitated
because installing LaTeX is a formidable task that requires a
lot of disk space.  I found that getting the APACHE web server
up and running on Linux is a cinch, and that loading Perl
CGIs isn't that hard.  I've written a Perl program that converts
S-Plus output with embedded narrative text coded as S-Plus
comments and with postscript graphics created using the Hmisc
library setps() function, to LaTeX, with pretty printing of
S-Plus code using the LaTeX S.sty style.  The students will
use our APACHE server to upload and automatically convert
such text files to PDF reports using LaTeX, dvips, and 
Ghostscript.  Interested persons should look at the documentation
and perhaps try the server at 
http://fharrell.biostat.virginia.edu/latex
(the server allows a limited number of simultaneous users).
The PDF documents created by the server use the LaTeX hyperref
style to hyperlink figures and other entities.  The above web
page has example input and .pdf output.

LaTeX is far superior to Microsoft Word for anything longer
than one page, and especially for making reproducible
statistical reports.  Organizations wishing to make using 
LaTeX easy for their staff might want to consider setting 
up a central LaTeX to PDF server as I've done.  
Anyone wanting a copy of my Perl CGI
program for running this application can drop me a line.  The
Perl script for doing the text conversion is already 
obtainable on the above web page.  The application only 
requires software that comes with many Linux distributions such as
RedHat (perl, latex, dvips, ghostscript).  I downloaded
the free world-class APACHE server from the web.

Note: Some of my students copy and paste graphics into the 
S-Plus 2000 Report window.  Sometimes this looks OK but
pagination and graph sizing can be difficult, and the resulting
file is very large.  Trellis graphics sometimes don't
turn out well using this approach. The setps( ) function with the
trellis=T option makes it easy to produce nicely-printing trellis
graphics as postscript.
-- 
