Yep.  S-PLUS can.

| I don't know much about using a C routine to
| do this type of task.  Is this a possible avenue?  Does anyone know
anything
| about other statistical packages like Mathcad or Matlad?
| 
| Any assistance would be greatly appreciated.  I don't want to grow old
| waiting for my results.

Many people fall into the trap that is clearly at the root of your dilemma.
Try to think of it this way.  gam() and friends are predominantly
exploratory tools for one data set at a time to be used "at the keyboard"
(or with the mouse, these days).  That's why they are so flexible and can
change models so easily, &c &c, but all of this comes at a price.  You
cannot really expect the same procedure to work well in a situation where
your task is entirely fixed beforehand, repeated literally thousands of
times and only a fraction of the work it is doing is going to be used,
anyway.  Your overheads are much too big: you are paying for something you
do not need and it is very costly.

What you have to do is pare down the calculation you want to do to its bare
essentials and use low-level tools for just those jobs that need to be done.
You could go to the C level if that's appropriate, but I suspect it will not
be.  You may have to be more cagey in how you go about your simulation as
well.  There are things like smooth.spline that can fit smoothing splines
quickly (if that is really what you should be doing - somehow I doubt it)
and tools like approx that can back solve in a way that may be entirely
adequate for your purposes, but without knowing more about your problem it
is almost impossible to give you any concrete advice.  The principle is
clear, though: you have to be more cunning in the way you use S-PLUS, there
are MANY levels on which it can be used and for your problem you need to go
much lower than you realise.  In short you need to become a real S
Programmer and not just an S-PLUS package user.  It isn't hard, trust me...
:-)

