From Michael.Wing at orst.edu  Tue Jan  9 01:35:22 2001
From: Michael.Wing at orst.edu (Wing, Michael)
Date: Mon, 08 Jan 2001 17:35:22 -0800
Subject: SUMMARY: Tree Summary
Message-ID: <6B677297622ED311A8E100902785F931D6DD33@ribes.for.orst.edu>

Thanks to Jean Adams for providing a nice solution to my problem.  Also,
thanks to D. McKenzie, Marc F. Feldesman, and Samuel Buttrey for offering
suggestions.  Here's the original question:

>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
I'm using S-Plus 2000 in an NT 4 environment.

I've been using regression trees and wish to create output that lists the
variables in their order of importance that were used in the creation of my
trees.  I plan on using this output for reporting purposes.

The summary function will produce output:

> summary(lglwdkp.fishlo1.prune8)

Regression tree:
snip.tree(tree = lglwdkp.fishlo1.tree, nodes = c(4, 11, 
        13, 24, 14, 10, 25, 15))
Variables actually used in tree construction:
[1] "OWNER"    "LANDUSE1" "BVRDAM01" "lgbldrs" 
[5] "CONSTRAN" "GRAD"    
Number of terminal nodes:  8 
Residual mean deviance:  0.39 = 89100 / 231000 
Distribution of residuals:
  Min. 1st Qu. Median   Mean 3rd Qu. Max. 
 -0.72   -0.29  -0.16 3e-014  -0.052  3.8

One of the problems I have with this output is that a variable may have been
used two or more times in the creation of the tree, but only appears once in
the output of "Variables actually used."  I would also like to only return
the variables as output and not the rest of the information that the summary
function generates.  Any ideas or solutions would be greatly appreciated.

>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>

 Here's Jean Adams' solution which did just what I needed:

The information you want is located in two places: the list of all the
variables used in tree building is in the frame component of the tree
objects, the "importance" of each of the variables (corresponding to the
height in a tree plot) is in the invisible output from a call to the
plot.tree() function.  A function like the following lists the variables
used in building the tree in their order of importance.

varlist <- function(tree.fit) {
    # list tree variables used in order of importance
    tree.sumry <- tree.fit$frame
    coords <- plot(tree.fit)
    tree.sumry.ord <- tree.sumry[order(coords$y), ]
    rev(as.character(tree.sumry.ord$var[tree.sumry.ord$var!="<leaf>"]))
    }

# try this out as an example ...
fit <- tree(Price ~ Length + Wheel.base + Width + Height, data=car.all,
na.action="na.exclude")
varlist(fit)

>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>

