In-Reply-To: <F61U4GALvtapufwbcm900011d44@hotmail.com>
References: <F61U4GALvtapufwbcm900011d44@hotmail.com>
Message-ID: <200101102236.OAA26566@tomato.statsci.com>

M.T Shakeri wrote:

>I am wondering, how can I draw a graph with following formula (Haar 
>function),
>
>
>  f(x)=-1/sqrt(2)   if -1 < x < 0
>      = 1/sqrt(2)   if  0 < x <=1
>      = 0           else where

Use ifelse() to define your function.

The usual way to graph a function is:
        x <- (some values over the right range)
        plot(x, f(x), type="l")

The following is useful for automating the graphic process:

Graph _ function(f, left = 0, right = 1, npoints = 81, ..., add = F, Par)
{
  ##Graph function f
  ##f may be given as an expression, in terms of x, but then ignore "..."
  ##Optional graphical parameters must go in list "Par"
  ##Had to move add argument after ... to avoid conflict with 
  ## optional argument "a" to f.
  ##This function chooses points between the limits
  cpoints _ function(xlim, n) {
    # Want high resolution near ends, but not right at ends
    xlim[1] + (xlim[2]-xlim[1]) * ( sin( pi/2 * (ppoints(n)*2 - 1)) + 1) /2
  }

  ## First determine the x limits, and x
  if( !missing(left) ) if( length(left)==2) {
    right _ left[2]
    left _ left[1]
  }

  ##If add==T & right/left unknown, then base x on old info
  ## If par()$usr is unchanged since last call to Graph,
  ##  then use old x, else use par()$user

  ## This function checks whether Graph.x exists & is consistent with
  ##  the current value of par("usr")
  check.Graph.x _ function() {
    if( exists("Graph.x",frame=0)) 
      if( all( par("usr") == get("Graph.x",frame=0)$usr )) return(T)
    F
  }

  if(add==T && missing(left) ){
    if( check.Graph.x() ) {
      x _ get("Graph.x",frame=0)$x
    } else {
      x _ cpoints( par("usr")[1:2], npoints )
    }
  } else {
    x _ cpoints( c(left,right), npoints )
  }

  ## Evaluate the function
  if(is.function(eval(substitute(f))))
    y _ f(x, ...)
  else
    y _ eval(substitute(f), local=sys.nframe() )
  ##See do.call() for the example of how this was created.

  ## Use plot() or lines() to graph the function
  if(!missing(Par)) {
    if(add) do.call("lines", c(list(x=x,y=y),Par))
    else do.call("plot",c(Par, list(x=x,y=y,xlab="y",ylab="x",type="l") ))
  }
  else{
    if(add) lines(x, y)
    else plot(x, y, type = "l", xlab="x", ylab="y")
  }

  assign("Graph.x", list( x=x, usr=par("usr")), frame=0)
  invisible(list(x=x,y=y))
}

Caveat - this is my own cade, not MathSoft code.  It has not gone
through any QA or other review here.  But I have used it succesfully
for years.

