From Bill.Venables at CMIS.CSIRO.AU  Fri Jan 12 04:29:22 2001
From: Bill.Venables at CMIS.CSIRO.AU
Date: Fri, 12 Jan 2001 14:29:22 +1000
Subject: RE: CART question
Message-ID: <2B7F04539DAFD311B61D009027D0DDA20C3BAD@roper-cv.qld.cmis.CSIRO.AU>

| -----Original Message-----
| From: Eric Wong [mailto:mcwong at cuhk.edu.hk]
| Sent: Friday, 12 January 2001 12:51
| To: s-news at lists.biostat.wustl.edu
| Subject: [S] CART question
| 
| 
| Dear all,
| 
| I tried to use CART to formula the length of stay (LOS) in 
| hospital with some risk factors.  In fact, LOS is a negatively 
| skewed variable.  I first tried a tree for LOS and the log(LOS), 
| and I found the results were totally different. 

I'm surprised the variable was negatively skewed.  With this kind of
response I would expect the distribution to be positively skewed, that is
with the "hump" near the origin and the heavy tail on the large positive
values. If it were negatively skewed the log transformation would not do
much for least squares techniques (other than make them worse).  Tree-based
models are fitted by least squares.

It's not surprising you get totally different looking trees for the cases
with and without log transforming the response, but how different are the
predictions (after back-transformation in the transformed case)?

| My questions are:
| 1) Should I take log(LOS) as it is skewed?

If it gives you a scale in which errors are more homogeneous, then probably
yes.  It does mean, though, that you are now considering relative errors
rather than absolute errors.  If the accuracy of your predictions is
naturally measured in relative terms (for example, 4%) rather than absolute
terms (for example, 4 days) then the log transformed response is the natural
one in which to work.

| 2) If yes, is it also applied to other non-parameteric methods such as
| GAM and MARS?

If your techinque is adding squares of errors you are clearly better off if
those errors are on a homogeneous scale, but there are competing
considerations, of course.  For example, with a gam you might want to
transform the response and fit an additive model or you may want to fit a
model in the original scale with a log link.  It depends on the context and
on what you want to do with the results.

Bill Venables.

