From dhk at paradox.psych.columbia.edu  Fri Jan 12 21:46:24 2001
From: dhk at paradox.psych.columbia.edu (Dave Krantz)
Date: Fri, 12 Jan 2001 16:46:24 -0500
Subject: difference between two predicted vectors
Message-ID: <200101122146.QAA15833@paradox.psych.columbia.edu>

Winnie Lambert writes:

> I have 2 vectors of probability forecasts (between 0 and 1) 500 elements
> long.  Each vector of forecasts was created using different forecast
> equations (same predictand but different types of predictors).  Their
> distributions are decidedly non-Gaussian.  After some reading I decided
> that the Wilcoxon signed-rank test is what I need to determine if the
> values in the vectors are significantly statistically different.  But,
> it states in the Help file that a Normal approximation will be used for
> the test statistic.  How can I interpret the output when that happens?

> Any thoughts on my choice of tests or sage advice?

I take it that the 2 forecast vectors differ ONLY in the equations
used -- that is, not only the predictands, but also the input data,
on which the forecasts are based, are the same.  Thus, we are talking
about a difference between two MODELS, not between two sets of data.
(I will assume that it makes sense to talk about identical input
data.)

In such a case, the question of "statistical significance" of the
difference between the two vectors has to be approached with great
care.

Either the two models make identical predictions, given identical
input data, or not.  (I assume the latter.)  Identical predictions
would show up most clearly on a scatterplot:  all points would
lie on the 45-degree line through the origin.  If any single point
deviates even slightly from that line, then the models ARE different,
and that difference is NOT due to chance, it is due to mathematics.
One might think that statistical tests of any kind are therefore
inappropriate for such a situation.  Of chief importance would be
a description of the nature of the differences between the models.

HOWEVER, I can conceive of a situation in which the above-mentioned
scatterplot looks quite noisy, with points varying irregularly about
the 45-degree line (or some curve).  In that case, it might be useful
to try to characterize the difference between the two models statistically,
by considering the joint distribution of predictions of the models,
across a population of prediction settings.  You might need to think
about the prediction settings actually used as a random sample from
some larger population of possible prediction settings; or something
of that sort, so that inference about the joint population distribution
can be based on the observed sample. 

The Wilcoxon test focuses on location difference between the two
marginal distributions.  Location difference is one of many possible
differences.  Unlike many situations, where location difference is
more important than anything else, it is not at all clear in this sort
of comparison that location difference has primary interest.  If you
concluded that there was no location difference, but a large difference
in scale, that would be a pretty vital difference between two models.

By the way, the normal approximation to the Wilcoxon distribution
has little relevance.  You seem here to be confusing the non-Gaussian
population distribution (of predictions) with a sampling distribution
(of the Wilcoxon test statistic).

"Sage advice" (or parsley, at least):

(1) Look at the scatterplot.

(2) Think the problem through a little better.  Does it make sense to
characterize the nonidentical paired predictions in terms of a joint
distribution?  Does it make sense to think of your predictions as
a random sample from some population?

(3) Don't confuse population distributions with sampling distributions.

Dave Krantz

