From swisdom at arbitrade.com  Fri Jan 12 23:43:08 2001
From: swisdom at arbitrade.com (Steve Wisdom)
Date: Fri, 12 Jan 2001 17:43:08 -0600
Subject: Tie-breaking in rank()
Message-ID: <C5E03A90949ED3118E7900A0C99D901F2E8432@pluto.arbitrade.com>

S+2000, PR3, on WinNT4

I`ve been vexed by the absence of a parameter for tie-break in function
rank(), and have a simple solution that may be helpful to others

The docs say `In case of ties, the average rank is returned.`

I would prefer to resolve ties using a rule of either 'pick first
occurrence' or 'pick last occurrence' as the tie-break

So I use the simple function ORDER to do this, as shown in the two versions
below

It utilizes the equivalence between rank(X) and order(order(X))

Note also that order(X) defaults to 'pick first occurrence', that is,
order(X,1:length(X)), though this isn`t explicit in the docs

Script. .. 

X_c(3,7,8,3,6,2,9,7)

ORDER_function(X) order(X,1:length(X),na.last=T)
cbind(X,RANK=rank(X,na.last=T),ORD.ORD=ORDER(ORDER(X)))

ORDER_function(X) order(X,length(X):1,na.last=T)
cbind(X,RANK=rank(X,na.last=T),ORD.ORD=ORDER(ORDER(X)))

Output. .. 

> X <- c(3, 7, 8, 3, 6, 2, 9, 7)
> ORDER <- function(X)
order(X, 1:length(X), na.last = T)
> cbind(X, RANK = rank(X, na.last = T), ORD.ORD = ORDER(ORDER(X)))
     X RANK ORD.ORD 
[1,] 3  2.5       2
[2,] 7  5.5       5
[3,] 8  7.0       7
[4,] 3  2.5       3
[5,] 6  4.0       4
[6,] 2  1.0       1
[7,] 9  8.0       8
[8,] 7  5.5       6
> ORDER <- function(X)
order(X, length(X):1, na.last = T)
> cbind(X, RANK = rank(X, na.last = T), ORD.ORD = ORDER(ORDER(X)))
     X RANK ORD.ORD 
[1,] 3  2.5       3
[2,] 7  5.5       6
[3,] 8  7.0       7
[4,] 3  2.5       2
[5,] 6  4.0       4
[6,] 2  1.0       1
[7,] 9  8.0       8
[8,] 7  5.5       5

