<< Could you please help with a stats question (Monte Carlo)?

Let's say I have N normally distributed variables V[1]...V[N] with means
u[1]...u[N] and a variance/covariancematrix.

I wish to generate a series of random trials (say 50,000) which match a
given
var/covar matrix (and the known means).

Generating N series of i.i.d. random normals is quite easy, but, of course,
the covariances will not match my desired covariances.

Do you have any thoughts? >>

From hussein at math.ualberta.ca  Mon Jan 15 04:36:52 2001
From: hussein at math.ualberta.ca (Abdulkadir Hussein)
Date: Sun, 14 Jan 2001 21:36:52 -0700
Subject: Re: simulation
In-Reply-To: <3f.f4807d4.2793a15d@aol.com>
References: <3f.f4807d4.2793a15d@aol.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.HPX.4.05.10101142103150.27332-100000@vega.math.ualberta.ca>

I am afraid I missed your point. But, if I did not then, you simply
need rmvnorm(N, mean=your.mean, cov=your.cov.matrix)
For instance (example from S-plus help), 
> rmvnorm(5, mean=c(9,3), cov=matrix(c(4,1,1,2), 2))
generates 5 i.i.d rows of  bivariate normal vectors each  of mean=[9 3]'
and covariance matrix
> cov
     [,1] [,2] 
[1,]    4    1
[2,]    1    2

But make sure that cov matrix is legitimate!, i.e., symmetric positive
definite

You can obtain further help by 
> ?rmvnorm

Abdul
