Hello,

Prabhakar Reddy Gudla Venkata Siva wrote:

> Say, i have an soils image ( 64x64 pixels) and i have the permeability
> values at each pixel. We can easily calculate the variogram and also the
> covariogram for this image ( i have done this by converting the image to a
> vector). Now what i intend to investigate is, if we do a DWT ( in 2d) on
> the same image using "HAAR" wavelets ( i have choosen this particular
> wavelet for simplicity of analysis ) can we somehow try to reproduce the
> covariogram/variogram (atleast approximately) from the coefficients of the
> DWT??
>
> I read something called SCALOGRAMS recently( "Analysis and Elucidation of
> soils variation using wavelets" by R.M.Lark and R.Webster, European
> Journal of Soil Science, June 1999, Vol 50, 185-206). And apart from
> the above cited paper, there seems to be few articles which refer to
> "Wavelet Variance" . So is there any way one could calculate the variogram
> atleast approximately from the coefficients of 2D DWT???

First, apologies for not responding more quickly. I only look at this list
infrequently.

You might be interested in the work of Idris Eckley here at Bristol who has/is
developed(ing) a wavelet-based "covariogram" for images. His web page is

http://www.stats.bris.ac.uk/~maiae/

His work is a significant development of the locally stationary wavelet process
work for time series undertaken by myself, Rainer von Sachs and Gerald Kroisandt
(J. Roy. Stat. Soc. Ser B, (2000), 62, 271--292). Software for the time series
version is available in the WaveThresh3 package

http://www.stats.bris.ac.uk/~wavethresh

I would imagine that Idris' software will eventually get into WaveThresh. He 
might
be willing to swap software for data :) This technology uses the non-decimated
wavelet transform and (to my mind at least) produces superior results when 
compared
to other scalogram methods that use similar wavelet transforms because of a
transformation in our work which guarantees unbiased (untangled) estimators.

There are quite a few other papers on "wavelet variance". Can I recommend you 
also
take a look at the nice work of Don Percival (University of Washington, 
Seattle),
Andrew Walden, Emma McCoy (both Imperial College, London) and Brandon Whitcher
(NCAR, Boulder). Their web pages are very informative. In particular, Don and
Andrew have a book which covers wavelet variance.

All the best,
Guy

