   We just put up 6.0, and are setting up local defaults.  One very nice
feature is the presence of options(character.to.factor=F) to turn off automatic
conversion of every character string to a factor.  Beyond this, one of our
tools for automatic import will need to be updated, raising the question of
whether one should use the 'character' or the 'string' class.  
   I know that Splus has chosen not to use strings as the default for 
almost anything -- but that may have been somewhat tied up with some bugs in
version 5.0 handling of string objects.  What are the current plusses and
minuses?

        Terry T.

From dsmith at statsci.com  Wed Jan 17 17:21:44 2001
From: dsmith at statsci.com (David M Smith)
Date: Wed, 17 Jan 2001 09:21:44 -0800
Subject: RE: version 6.0 and strings
In-Reply-To: <200101171642.KAA11385@rocky.mayo.edu>
References: <200101171642.KAA11385@rocky.mayo.edu>
Message-ID: <LGEFIDJJDBKMHCAKBCOIKENHCIAA.dsmith@splus.mathsoft.com>

Sv4 strings aren't officially supported in S-PLUS 6.0 for Unix or Windows.
String objects aren't discussed in the S-PLUS documentation, and the on-line
help page for string() states "Function string is not supported in this
version. Its use is discouraged".  The reason for this is that string
support in the engine isn't yet production quality (and strings aren't
supported in either the Windows or Unix GUIs at all).

If you read the Green Book, John Chambers does discuss strings in S.  In
S-PLUS all the functions are there, but essentially hidden from the user.
(This is just one of the many differences between S and S-PLUS.)  None of
the other system functions make use of string objects.  So for now, the
advice is to stick with characters.

We do plan to support string objects in a later release of S-PLUS (but not
6.0).

