> Dear SPlus-Users,
>
> I have a data frame of dimension 119, 3 with a binary variable (Y) as
> response, with one variable (X1) as explanatory variable and a dummy
> variable (X2) as grouping variable. With 60 of the records ([X2==1]),
> I fitted a simple generalized linear model, with the other 59 records
> ([X2==0]), I tried to validate this simple model:
>
> >  log.glm <- glm(Y[X2==1] ~ X1[X2==1], family=binomial(link=logit),
> >na.action=na.omit)
> >  log.glm.pred <- predict.glm(log.glm,
> >newdata=as.data.frame(X1[X2==0]), type="response")
>
> The following message appears: "Problem in model.matrix.default(Š
> Length of variable 1 is 60 != length of row names(59))".
> If I use in predict.glm for X1 a vector of length 60 (e.g. X1 <-
> rep(0,60)), I get the same predictions, as if I would use the
> calibration data X1[X2==1].
>
> Where is actually the problem? Thanks for any reply in advance!

First, these things are much easier if you use data frames as intended.
I assume you have one attached since you mention one.

df <- data.frame(Y = runif(119) > 0.7, X1 = rnorm(119),
                 X2 = c(rep(1, 60), rep(0, 59)))

log.glm <- glm(Y ~ X1, family=binomial, data=df, subset = X2 == 1)
predict.glm(log.glm, df[df$X2 == 0, ], type="response")

works.  I do suggest that you look up some examples in a good reference.

Now, let's show you what you had wrong.  You didn't tell me your version
of S-PLUS: this is 3.4.

attach(df)
log.glm <- glm(Y[X2==1] ~ X1[X2==1], family=binomial)
log.glm
Coefficients:
 (Intercept) X1[X2 == 1]
  -0.6162063   0.1969958
# Ugly, isn't it?
predict.glm(log.glm, newdata=as.data.frame(X1[X2==0]), type="response")
Error in model.matrix.default(delete.response(Term..: Length of variable 1
is 60 != length of row names (59)

options(error=dump.frames)
predict.glm(log.glm, newdata=as.data.frame(X1[X2==0]), type="response")
> debugger()
Message: Length of variable 1 is 60 != length of row names (59)

1:
2: predict.glm(log.glm, newdata = as.data.frame(X1[X2 == 0]), type =
"response")
3: ".F3"(NextMethod("predict"))
4: care.exp(eta)
5: predict.lm(object, newdata = newdata, type = type)
6: model.matrix.default(delete.response(Terms), newdata, contrasts =
object$
7: model.frame.default(terms.object, data)
8: model.matrix(delete.response(Terms), newdata, contrasts =
object$contrasts)
Selection: 6
d(6)> terms.object
expression(X1[X2 == 1])
attr(, "formula"):
 ~ X1[X2 == 1]
attr(, "factors"):
            X1[X2 == 1]
X1[X2 == 1]           1
attr(, "order"):
[1] 1
attr(, "variables"):
expression(X1[X2 == 1])
attr(, "term.labels"):
[1] "X1[X2 == 1]"
attr(, "intercept"):
[1] 1
attr(, "response"):
[1] 0
attr(, "class"):
[1] "terms"

So, as you see, the explanatory variable in your model is X1[X2 == 1],
not X1.
d(6)> 0
...
d(5)> names(newdata)
[1] "x"
...

Exercise: work out where X1[X2 == 1] comes from and what values it has.
Certainly not from newdata.

So: (a) You did not give a useful newdata and (b) you did not realize that
the subsetting [X2 == 1] had become part of the model, to be used in
predction.

