Fredrik Lundgren asks:

| From: Fredrik Lundgren
| [mailto:fredrik.lundgren at norrkoping.mail.telia.com]
| Sent: Sunday, 21 January 2001 3:24
| To: s-news at lists.biostat.wustl.edu
| Subject: [S] Hierarchical sampling
| 
| 
| Dear S-experts,
| 
| I have a problem with hierarchical sampling
| 
| # I have a matrix 'prov' 
| 
| > prov
|    group gold test 
|  1     1    1    1
|  2     1    0    1
|  3     2    1    1
|  4     2    0    0
|  5     3    1    0
|  6     3    0    0
|  7     4    1    1
|  8     4    0    0
|  9     5    0    0
| 10     5    1    0
| 11     6    0    1
| 12     6    1    1
| 13     7    0    0
| 14     7    0    0
| 15     8    0    0
| 16     8    0    0
| 17     9    0    0
| 18     9    0    0
| 19    10    1    0
| 20    11    0    0
| 
| # Groups aren't necessarily of the same size - as seen for 
| group 10 and 11
| 
| 
| # I sample with replacement on group level
| 
| n <- length(unique(prov[,'group']))
| s <-sample(n, n, replace=T)

Why not just sample from the groups themselves:

grp <- unique(prov[, 'group'])
sam <- sample(grp, length(grp), replace = T)

(It does not make any difference here, I know, but it would if 
 your groups were not labelled by the integers 1, 2, ..., n)

| 
| > s
|  [1] 2 5 2 1 9 7 7 8 8 4
| 
| # Now I would like to form a new matrix with data from groups 
| as they were sampled
| 
| new.matrix
| 
|  group gold test
|  1     2    1    1
|  2     2    0    0
|  3     5    0    0
|  4     5    1    0
|  5     2    1    1
|  6     2    0    0
|  7     1    1    1
|  8     1    0    1
|  9     9    0    0
| 10     9    0    0
| 11     7    0    0
| 12     7    0    0
| 13     7    0    0
| 14     7    0    0
| 15     8    0    0
| 16     8    0    0
| 17     8    0    0
| 18     8    0    0
| 19     4    1    1
| 20     4    0    0
| 
| # How do I generate such a new.matrix???

Here's one way.

# find out where the groups are located within the matrix:

where <- split(1:nrow(prov), prov[, 'group'])

# now piece together the index vector you need:

i <- unlist(where[sam])

# finally, get your matrix

new.matrix <- prov[i, ]

# done.  But if you want it all in one step...

new.matrix <- prov[unlist(split(1:nrow(prov), prov[, 'group'])
       [sample(unique(prov[,'group']), replace=T)]), ]

| 
| I use S-Plus 2000 rel 2

OK, but this should work anywhere.

I love this kind of stuff!  :-)

