From remigijus.lapinskas at maf.vu.lt  Tue Jan 23 16:08:52 2001
From: remigijus.lapinskas at maf.vu.lt (Remigijus Lapinskas)
Date: Tue, 23 Jan 2001 17:08:52 +0100
Subject: initial values in nls
Message-ID: <17714.010123@maf.vu.lt>

Here is my (slightly edited) original problem and responses from
Douglas Bates and Nick Ellis (I thank both of them):

When starting nonlinear regression, one has to indicate the initial
values of the coefficients. How can I do this in
FFF <- data.frame(x, y)
param(FFF, "a1") <- aa1
param(FFF, "a2") <- aa2
...................
param(FFF, "a6") <- aa6
fit <- nls(y ~ exp(poly(x,5))
where, in the formula, there is no place for parameters?!
I can't take logarithms of both sides because of some zeros in y.

**********************
A response from Douglas Bates
**********************

You will need to be more careful in expressing your model.  For nls
the right hand side of the formula must include the parameters but
the parameters a1, a2, ..., a6 do not appear in exp(poly(x, 5)).

You could use exp(poly(x,5) %*% c(a1,a2,a3,a4,a5,a6)) but that may
complicate getting starting estimates because all the rows in
poly(x,5) change if you delete any row.  I would suggest using an
explicit representation of the polynomial as

y ~ exp(((((a6*x+a5)*x+a4)*x+a3)*x+a2)*x+a1)

to get starting estimates you could use

coef(lm(log(y) ~ outer(x, 0:5, "^"), data = FFF, subset = y > 0))

so you only take the logarithm of the positive values.

Another possibility is to use glm with family = poisson to get a log
link.

*************************
A response from Nick Ellis
*************************

poly(x,5) actually returns a matrix of polynomials and so is
suitable for passing to linear models. For non-linear
models, you have to evaluate the function explicitly, thus:

x <- rep(-5:5,100)
y <- exp(x+rnorm(x)/10)
FFF <- data.frame(x, y)
param(FFF, "alpha") <- log(mean(y))
param(FFF, "beta") <- rep(0,5)
fit <- nls(y ~ exp(alpha+poly(x,5)%*%beta), data=FFF)
plot(x,y)
lines(sort(x),predict(fit)[order(x)])

An alternative to using param is to specify start in the argument list:

fit <- nls(y ~ exp(alpha+poly(x,5)%*%beta), data=FFF,
start=list(alpha=log(mean(y)),beta=rep(0,5)))

Best,
Remigijus

