From chris at psyctc.org  Wed Jan 24 11:17:37 2001
From: chris at psyctc.org (Chris Evans)
Date: Wed, 24 Jan 2001 11:17:37 +0000
Subject: bootstrapping or jackknifing rotated PCA/factor analyses
Message-ID: <3A6EB9D1.26364.9B4A0E9@localhost>

A colleague has a small n (n=25) study of six variables.  The 
variables are of good reliability and there is some a priori sense in 
which you would expect that three would covary and others also 
covary but that there would be a difference between the two 
packets of three.  (There is no sense in which a strict a priori 
covariance matrix or loading matrix can be specified and tested 
using CFA.)

Rotated PCA shows a very striking pattern of a first component on 
which all six load strongly and positively and a second component 
on which there is a clear bipolar loading.  I'm arguing that the 
numbers are very small but that the result does look worthy of 
publication (given theoretical considerations etc.) but only if we can 
put some standard errors on the eigenvalues (and hence the 
decision to rotate two components) and on the loadings.

My recollection is that there was a literature on bootstrapping and 
jackknifing for PCA etc.  My recollection is that there are some 
serious issues about using something like a procrustes rotation to 
rotate the generated samples toward the original sample pattern 
and some issues about radical simplification of the formulae and 
hence the computation for jackknifing. Can someone point me to a 
good current summary and has anyone implemented anything to 
do this in S+? 

TIA,

Chris

