On 24 Jan 2001, at 14:03, Kim Elmore wrote:

> Hi, Chris.
Hi!
> 
> Your note about the loadings reminded me of something:
> 
> You mention that the second PC shows a bipolar characteristic.  I believe 
> that this is *not* interpretable, because it is an artifact of the topology 
> of the eigenspace. This is how the loadings must behave in order to meet 
> the requirement that the first PC explain the most variance, while the 
> second explains the next largest amount of variance, etc.  In almost all 
> cases, the first PC is unimodal, and the second is bimodal.  What happens 
> in the higher PC's is a function of the domain dimensionality.  In your 
> case, I'm not sure how to characterize this, but in almost *every* PCA, the 
> first PC is unimodal while the second is bimodal.  If you want to 
> *interpret* the results, you'll probably need to  perform a rotation.  I 
> find Varimax to be a good start.
I should have said that it was after varimax.  However, it is 
precisely the problem you mention, or, to be more precise (I think) 
the problem that this is what you often see if you have "over-
extracted" (I think to some extent it tends to occur whether you 
rotate or not, gets more distinct if you rotate?)

> For a more in-depth look at this (albeit, NOT from a psychological 
> standpoint) see:
> 
> Richman, M. B., 1986: Rotation of principal components. International J. 
> of     Climatology, 6, 293-335, Roy. Meteor. Soc.
> 
> This paper contains references to other discussions about eigenspace topology.
That's one of the ones I think I did know.  Certainly Richman is one 
of the people I read and seem to remember he answered a 
question of mine very helpfully some ages back.  However, my 
questions about jackknifing and bootstrapping, which seem to me 
to be some of the ways of addressing the sampling/precision 
issues, stand (I think!)

Cheers all,

Chris
