From dmck at u.washington.edu  Fri Jan 26 01:19:36 2001
From: dmck at u.washington.edu (D. Mckenzie)
Date: Thu, 25 Jan 2001 17:19:36 -0800
Subject: Re: bootstrapping or jackknifing rotated PCA/factor analyses
In-Reply-To: <3A70C85F.18804.F0B77F@localhost>
References: <3A70C85F.18804.F0B77F@localhost>
Message-ID: <Pine.A41.4.21.0101251710520.65748-100000@mead4.u.washington.edu>

Allow me to open my big mouth again about the difference between PCA and
factor analysis.  Factor analysis estimates loadings, which, in linear
combinations with "unseen factors", produce the observed variables.  In
contrast, in PCA the principal components are linear combinations of
estimated loadings with the variables, thus a rotation is implicit.

What Richman and others call "rotated principal components" is really
factor analysis, which by following the math in his very lucid 1986 paper
one can confirm.  The issue is further muddied because one estimation
method for factor analysis is called the "principal component" method.  I
believe this method produces loadings that arte proportional to those that
would be produced by a factor analysis.

My $.02 for 2001.

