Thanks!  I am a psychotherapist, not a mathematician so I guess I 
should just accept what you say, but I thought the difference was 
that a PCA assumes no specific "factors" but merely reorganises 
the variance by producing linear combinations of the observed 
variables: no estimation, no sampling model, just reorganisation 
and thus a unique "rotated" solution: rotated to get most possible 
variance on 1st PCA, next most in the orthogonal 2nd ...

Now, when you rotate that, you get a can of worms and still not a 
factor analysis, but it's what a lot of us do 'cos it's simple.  My 
understanding was that a number of people have done simulation 
work showing that for many common scenarios, but not all, the 
results from PCA and one of the factor analytic methods are very, 
very similar.

However, a factor analysis assumes specific and common factors 
and partions the variance and uses various methods, some of 
which (ML?) have a clear sampling model (?) in doing so.

I think aspects of the maths, which is beyond me, enable one to 
greatly simplify the computation of a jackknife for some things 
