Subject: Re: Enhanced Box Plot
Message-ID: <993D57BFC276D311A4220008C7A48E2D066D7A@es09snlnt.sandia.gov>

I was able to modify the 'panel.bwplot' function to print the number of
observations inside the plot area. Is there a way to print this information
along the right side axis in the graph area?

Thanks, Greg Snow, Madeline Bauer, Don MacQueen, and Joel Dubin, for your
responses.

Calvin
Email: cking at sandia.gov

My Question:
        I work with datasets where boxplots are a natural choice and I am
looking at
        encoding more information than is achieved using the available
Trellis
        graphics function, bwplot, which BTW is excellent to begin with.
        Let's say we create a box plot thus:
                bwplot(MyFactor~MyNumeric, data=MySample)
        'MySample' has 1000 rows, and 'MyFactor' has 6 distinct levels. Let
count1,
        count2, count3, count4, count5, count6 be the number of rows that
belong to
        each of the 6 levels of 'MyFactor'.
        Has anyone looked at displaying the value of countX either by
annotating
        next to the corresponding box plot, or by scaling the width/height
of the
        box in proportion to the value of countX?

Greg Snow:
        The non-trellis function boxplot has an option (varwidth) that will
make
        the widths of the boxes be proportional to sqrt(n), I believe the
boxplots
        made by the gui also have an option for that under the boxspecs
page.
        I don't know how to do it using the trellis function (bwplot)
without
        writing your own panel function.

Madeline Bauer:
        Yes ... John Tukey (may he rest in peace), the inventor of the box
plot,
        suggested that it might be informative to have the width (which
would be
        the height, in the trellis form) of the box proportional to the
square root
        of the number of observations. This is an option in the non-trellis
boxplot
        function with varwidth=T. Now, if only the code for boxplot were
viewable,
        but it doesn't seem to be. Perhaps you can figure out how to modify
        panel.bwplot or fiddle with the parameter box.ratio ??

Don Macqueen:
        I have a simple extension of the boxplot function (not the Trellis 
        version) that adds counts to the boxplot labels. 
        # 4/30/98
        # version of boxplot that adds n to the labels
        #
        boxpl <- function(x,...) {
           bx <- boxplot(x,plot=F,...)
           bx$names <- paste(bx$names,'\nn = ',bx$n,sep='')
           bxp(bx)
           invisible(bx)
        }

Joel Dubin:
        J. Jack Lee (from M.D. Anderson Cancer Center) and I wrote a
        function several years back to add features to the existing boxplot
        function at that time.  This is a non-Trellis function.  Features
include 
        sorting categories for the plots to your liking, as well as stating
the 
        sample size under each boxplot and an indicator for the mean inside
each
        boxplot region.

