From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Tue Jan 30 07:49:01 2001
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Tue, 30 Jan 2001 07:49:01 +0000
Subject: Re: I am having trouble in calling Splus from C / Java
In-Reply-To: <000e01c08b35$822eb300$0d01a8c0@bharat.effigent.com>
References: <000e01c08b35$822eb300$0d01a8c0@bharat.effigent.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.31.0101300739490.17104-100000@auk.stats>

On Tue, 30 Jan 2001, ulag wrote:

> Hi,
>
> I have just started on Splus and having the following problem.
>
> I have several splus scripts. All these takes different arguments and these 
> arguments are know only at the run time. Hence, I wanted a generic C function 
> from which I could generate the splus function name, and  all the arguments 
> to call_S function.
>
> When I was going through the Splus documentation, I came to the understanding 
> that, I can't use call_S directly from a C program. Is this true?. And if so, 
> is there any way, I can  call splus scripts with the arguments from with in a 
> C program?.
>
> Any suggestions would be greatly appreciated

Starting by writing call_S is pretty ambitious!

Do you actually want to call S scripts or S functions?  They are
different things.  You do need an S engine running, so you can only
call S functions from within C (by call_S or .Call) from C code
loaded into S-PLUS.  (This will change with S-PLUS 6.0 for Windows,
where you can call S functions from C++, but not without some work.)

If you want to call S scripts, use a system call to write the approriate
S command-line with indirection, e.g.

Splus < infile > outfile  [Unix]
Sqpe < infile > outfile   [Windows]

....

At that point I am going to stop guessing, and remind you to tell what OS
and S-PLUS version you want to use, and that is normally better to
describe the real problem you want to solve, not a non-functioning
solution.   That long-term S-PLUS users very rarely meet anything like this
problem might be a clue to you.

Please wrap the lines in your messages, or get your mail agent to do so.

