From lane.bishop at honeywell.com  Tue Jan 30 13:32:42 2001
From: lane.bishop at honeywell.com (Bishop, Lane)
Date: Tue, 30 Jan 2001 06:32:42 -0700
Subject: RE: RE: AR process correlations
Message-ID: <F1AEDCE9021ED3118E0E0008C79FA7A0466B29@bufex167.buf.allied.com>

Peter,

The exact expression for an AR process of order p can be found in Box,
Jenkins and Reinsel, Time Series Analysis, in section 3.2.2 on page 55.  It
will be also in the first edition in a similar place, I'm sure, but my copy
of that is at home.  The k-th autocorrelation is a linear combination of the
k-th powers of the p roots of the characteristic equation,
   1 - phi(1)*B - phi(2)*B^2 - ... - phi(p)* B^p = 0
(phi is alpha in your notation).

You estimate the phi's in S-Plus using something like ar() or arima.mle().

The specific solutions for 2nd order (p=2) are discussed in section 3.2.4
("Second-Order Autoregressive Process"), where a nice closed form expression
is given.  If you can't easily get the book, let me know and I'll type it
out for you.

I am definitely not a wiz at S-Plus time series functions.  However, I seem
to remember that there is a function that calculates the autocorrelation
function from a fitted model.  Anybody else know?  Either that or I wrote
one once - I'll look.

  - Lane

Ah, found it!  Here you go...  The following calculates the autocorrelation
function from the recursion relation given in Box, Jenkins and Reinsel.  Get
the phi values as I mentioned from a time series fit in S-Plus.

#  ar.acf - Calculate theoretical acf of given AR model
#         - See Box, Jenkins & Reinsel page 56, equation 3.2.4 or 3.2.6

ar.acf <- function( phi, nlags=length(phi) )
{
    p <- length( phi )
    L1 <- c( phi, rep( 0, p ) )
    L2 <- c( rep( 0, p ), phi )
    M1 <- M2 <- Iden <- diag( length(phi) ) # right dimensions
    for( i in 1:p ) {
        M1[ i , ] <- L1[ (i+1):(i+p) ]
        M2[ , i ] <- L2[ (p+1-i):(p+p-i) ]
    }
    rho <- solve( Iden - M1 - M2, phi )
    if( nlags > length(rho) ) {
        rho <- c( rho, rep( 0, nlags-length(rho) ) )
        for( k in (length(phi)+1):length(rho) )
            rho[k] <- sum( phi * rho[(k-1):(k-p)] )
    }
    else rho <- rho[ 1:nlags ]
    rho
}

