S-users:

a) I solved my own problem.  Thanks to anyone who gave it some thought.

b) More importantly I learned something important about the lmList (and
similar functions):

There were other columns in my data set (sheep1.fix) that contained
numerous NA's. In fact one column contained NA's for every measurement
from sheep 3.

The key point is that na.action=na.omit does not just eliminate rows when
the variables used are NA, it eliminates any row in which there is an NA
(even if that row is not being used!).

Example:

x_rep(1:        5,4)
group_sort(rep(1:       5,4))
y_rnorm(group+2*x+rnorm(20))
miss_sample(c(1,NA),20,replace=T)
        
junk<- data.frame(x,y,group,miss)

> junk
   x           y group miss 
 1 1 -1.80726536     1   NA
 2 2 -2.61520830     1    1
 3 3 -0.18790145     1   NA
 4 4 -0.13910055     1    1
 5 5 -0.06089259     2    1
 6 1  0.34434902     2   NA
 7 2 -2.09778420     2   NA
 8 3 -1.14091719     2   NA
 9 4  1.90571136     3    1
10 5  0.25632422     3   NA
11 1  1.56097070     3   NA
12 2  0.33637390     3    1
13 3  1.33265693     4   NA
