From rolf at math.unb.ca  Tue Jan 30 19:03:08 2001
From: rolf at math.unb.ca (Rolf Turner)
Date: Tue, 30 Jan 2001 15:03:08 -0400
Subject: Re: Confidence Interval on Quotient
Message-ID: <200101301903.PAA17091@tanner.math.unb.ca>

Bootstrapping isn't necessary, at least under some ``usual''
assumptions --- normality and homoscedasticity.

Suppose X_1, ..., X_n1 ~ N(mu_1,sigma^2);
        X_1, ..., X_n2 ~ N(mu_2,sigma^2)  (same sigma).

Let rho = mu_1/mu_2.

To test the hypothesis H_0: rho = rho_0 we can use the test statistic

                       (X-bar - rho_0 * Y-bar)
                  T = --------------------------
                      s_p*sqrt(1/n1 + rho_0^2/n2)

which will have a t distribution with n1+n2-2 degrees of freedom
under H_0.  (Note: s_p = pooled estimate of sigma.)

A confidence interval for rho may be formed as the set of all
rho_0 for which H_0 would NOT be rejected.  The boundaries of
this interval can be found by solving the quadratic equation

                T^2 = (table value)^2

for rho_0.  It's not clear to me whether the confidence region
will indeed be an interval --- it could be the exterior or
complement of an interval; some experimentation should clear
this up.

If heteroscedasticity is a problem then you are in a Behrens-Fisher
bind, which may be solvable via the Welch-Aspin-Smith-Satterthwaite
approach as is used in the usual setting of inference about mu_1 - mu_2.
(I haven't thought this through.)

If non-normality is a problem then bootstrapping is probably the
only way to go.  But this is only a worry if n1 and n2 are small.
And if this is the case then you're in trouble no matter what you
do. :-)

If n1 and n2 are large then neither heteroscedasticity nor
non-normality should be a problem.  (Just take df = infinity.)

                                        cheers,

                                                Rolf Turner
                                                rolf at math.unb.ca

From dhk at paradox.psych.columbia.edu  Tue Jan 30 18:59:04 2001
From: dhk at paradox.psych.columbia.edu (Dave Krantz)
Date: Tue, 30 Jan 2001 13:59:04 -0500
Subject: Re: Confidence Interval on Quotient
Message-ID: <200101301859.NAA27504@paradox.psych.columbia.edu>

I've answered this question many times in different places, so I'll
just attach my standard answer below:  it gives a first and
a second order approximation for the coefficient of variation
of the ratio between two means.  If your sample sizes are large
enough to make bootstrapping reliable, then most likely the
approximations here will be good ones that will save you the
trouble of bootstrapping.

As you will note, the answer I give is based on Taylor series,
and is only valid for situations where the coefficient of variation
of the denominator is relatively small ( < 20%).  If the
denominator is small and highly variable, the ratio measure
will be extremely skewed positively in any case, so the standard
error of it is not such a useful quantity, and the whole notion
of taking ratios in such a case is somewhat dubious.

        -------------------------------------------------

The Taylor series for  R = A/B,  through its first two orders,
looks like this:

dR ~ (1/B)*dA - (A/B^2)*dB + (A/B^3)*dB^2 - (1/B^2)*dA*dB       (EQ 1)

(there is no term for dA^2 since the second partial derivative of R
with respect to A is 0).
In the application here, dA and dB can be considered independent
random variables with mean 0 (departures of sample mean from population
mean for the two samples--it is important that the samples are
independent, not important whether they come from the same population).

If one squares EQ 1, takes expectations, uses independence and mean 0,
and simplifies, one gets the following second-order approximation,
in which cvA, cvB, and cvR denote the coefficients of variation of A, B, R
(i.e., cvA = SA/A, etc.), B3 denotes the normalized third central moment
of the denominator B (expectation of the third power of the deviation
from the mean, divided by the third power of the mean), and B4 denotes
the analogous normalized fourth central moment of B:

cvR ~ sqrt(cvA^2 + cvB^2 + (cvA*cvB)^2 - 2*B3 + B4)             (EQ 2)

If the central limit theorem applies approximately to statistic B,
so its distribution is approximately Gaussian, EQ 2 can be simplified:
B3 is essentially 0 and B4 is approximately 3*(cvB^4).  So for this case,
the second-order approximation becomes

cvR ~ sqrt(cvA^2 + cvB^2 + (cvA*cvB)^2 + 3*(cvB^4))             (EQ 3)

If cvA and cvB are less than 20%, this is a quite good approximation;
and if they are less than 10%, the latter two terms in it play little
role--the first order approximation, essentially Pythagorean combination
of coefficients of variation is pretty good.  However, this second-order
approximation goes to pot rapidly for coefficients of variation
over 20%--one needs higher terms of the Taylor expansion and it
may no longer be worthwhile to look for any formula unless you have
particularly interesting special cases.

Dave Krantz

