Message-ID: <GPECKMJDPHIDALEELIMHCECECBAA.ezivot@u.washington.edu>

The Fieller method (see also Rao's linear models textbook) is definitely the
method of choice in this situation as the delta method (Taylor series
approximation) can be particularly bad if the denominator mean is anywhere
close to zero. The Fieller method produces an exact test and the test can be
inverted to form a confidence interval. However, the confidence interval may
look strange in some cases. In fact, if the denominator mean is not
statistically different from zero at the 5% level then the Fieller
confidence set is an unbounded interval (either [-00,00] or [-00, L] U [U,
00]). This is appropriate since the ratio of the two means is not defined if
the denominator is zero.
ez

